  "name": "Question 11",
  "problem": "Let $V=C[-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}],$ the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$ Find $\u27e8{sin}^{2}\u2061t,\\operatorname{cos}\u2061t\u27e9$ if the inner product on $V$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\u27e8x(t),y(t)\u27e9={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "We need to evaluate\n\n$$\n\u27e8{sin}^{2}\u2061t,\\operatorname{cos}\u2061t\u27e9={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}\u2061t\\operatorname{cos}\u2061t\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this integral using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t,$ $\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{cos}\u2061t\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}\u2061t\\operatorname{cos}\u2061t\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\frac{1}{3}{u}^{3}|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{3}(1)^{3}]-[\\frac{1}{3}(-1)^{3}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
